  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "description": "<div>\n<p>For each whole number from 0 through n, return how many 1 digits appear in its binary representation. The answer at index i must describe i, including the zero at index 0.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 2\n<strong>Output:</strong> [0,1,1]\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Zero has no set bits, while 1 and 2 each have one.</pre>\n<p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 5\n<strong>Output:</strong> [0,1,1,2,1,2]\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> The binary forms of 3 and 5 each have two set bits.</pre>\n<p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 0\n<strong>Output:</strong> [0]\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Even the zero-length range above zero includes index 0.</pre>\n<p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>0 &lt;= n &lt;= 1000</code>.</li>\n<li>Return an array containing exactly <code>n + 1</code> nonnegative counts.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>",
